    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup centers around **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who commands an elite score of 87.22, far and away our strongest play. Despite facing a formidable arm in Cole, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a high-leverage No. 2 spot, favorable wind conditions, and a strong BvP track record (33 PA), which collectively buoy his efficiency score toward the \"Strong\" category. While most of our lineup is currently grinding through moderate matchups, **Taylor Ward** serves as a vital secondary engine; his 49.36 score is significantly bolstered by a favorable park factor (+16%) and the leadoff advantage (+10%), helping to offset the difficulty of facing Gilbert.\n\nOur bench decision-making was particularly tight today, as **Matt Chapman** (40.14) just narrowly missed the cut behind **Jung Hoo Lee** (40.01). While **Matt Chapman** technically holds a higher raw projection, the internal model favored **Jung Hoo Lee** due to the specific combination of the wind blowing out and the order context, despite the presence of an elite catcher behind the plate. Meanwhile, **Luis Arraez**, **Bryson Stott**, **Geraldo Perdomo**, and **Masyn Winn** are all functioning as high-floor volume plays today; specifically, keep an eye on **Masyn Winn**, whose +2% bump for facing a pitcher with a slow delivery provides an incremental edge in our hunt for stolen bases.\n\nWe are operating with a cautious approach today, as several starters are facing xERAs near or below the 3.20 mark, indicating tough sledding against high-tier arms. **Gleyber Torres** faces a minor risk with wind blowing in (-5%), and we continue to monitor the status of **Brendan Donovan**, **Daulton Varsho**, and **Tyler Freeman** as we evaluate depth options to preserve our remaining game caps. With **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz** both sidelined on the IL, our roster flexibility is limited; we are relying heavily on the daily consistency of **Gavin Sheets** and the prospect depth of **Dylan 